<section id="about" class="part">
<h2>What this page is</h2>
<p>The <code>fortress-repo</code> skill holds the rules that bind every worker in the repository. This page shows each change that landed in it, as a diff with its reason, so that the curator can check what the workers are now told at any time. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on its item, or say so in chat.</p>
</section>

<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2>Not yet read</h2>
<p>Seventy-nine items, none of them read. The page holds only what the curator has not read. At 07:45 UTC on 8 November, the curator&rsquo;s comment on change 38 marked changes 37 and 38 read, so they left, and the page starts at change 39. No comment marks a later item read.</p>
<p>Climb batch 27 landed on <code>main</code>, and its landing made some sentences of the skill false. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 16:18 and 16:28 UTC on 7 November. Changes 39 to 41 come from the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: the update rewrote three of its sections for the landing, its judge said to install all three, and the skill writer copied them in. Changes 42 to 47 bring <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>tests-writing.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> in line with what the batch changed in walk, the library and the code generator. A cold read of these changes then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed most of them. Each item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
<p>Change 48 came after the landing, at the curator&rsquo;s word in chat. The skill writer made it in two commits to <code>committing.md</code>, at 16:42 and 16:48 UTC on 7 November, the second with the fixes after its cold read.</p>
<p>On 8 November, the curator&rsquo;s comments on changes 37 and 39 led to two more commits. At the curator&rsquo;s word of 01:07 UTC, the skill writer made the fixes in one commit at 01:09 UTC. A cold read then found passages that would send a worker wrong, and the skill writer fixed them in a second commit at 02:06 UTC. The two commits changed three passages. One is the passage of change 39, which now shows these changes too, with a second reason, &ldquo;Why, 8 November&rdquo;. Another, in <code>SKILL.md</code>, is change 49. The third is the passage of change 37, which the curator has since read.</p>
<p>Climb batch 28 landed on <code>main</code> on 8 November, and its landing made more sentences of the skill false or incomplete. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 11:23, 11:27 and 11:36 UTC. Changes 50 and 51 come from the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install two of its sections, and the skill writer copied them in. Changes 52 to 57 bring <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>records.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> in line with the landing, and with two findings of the batch&rsquo;s review on how the workers worked: a shallow clone, and a timed run that measured one commit twice. The landing also changed the passage of change 43, which now shows it, with a second reason, &ldquo;Why, climb batch 28&rdquo;. Changes 58 to 67 come from cuts. <code>compiler.md</code> had grown to 30,491 characters, past 30,000, the size past which a worker&rsquo;s <code>cat</code> of the part is saved to a file and read again, as two agents of the batch had to do. To bring it under, the skill writer cut text that states no rule, and it is now 29,999 characters. Change 58 defines a term whose pointer a cut took away, and changes 59 to 67 are the cuts. A cold read of these changes then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed all of them. Each item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
<p>Five items of climb batch 27 were new rules when they landed: change 39, a type without its own <code>=</code> compares by strict equivalence; change 40, the static argument written where walk's printed type misleads; change 41, <code>Number</code> declares nothing; change 42, walk's float numerals under strict equivalence; change 48, a commit already made whose footer names a model is left as it is. The other five from that batch state facts that it changed, and change no rule. Change 49 is not a new rule either: it defines two terms. Nor are the changes of 8 November to change 39: it now spells out strict equivalence and three cases where a worker would guess wrong.</p>
<p>On the afternoon of 8 November, the curator read changes 39 to 44 and refused them: they wrote the behaviour of one implementation as rules of the language, and told workers to assert it as correct. The curator&rsquo;s rule: <code>revival-changes.md</code> describes the language of revived Fortress and the revival&rsquo;s changes against the team&rsquo;s design, and a behaviour of one path that the language does not give is a defect, with a ledger row, noted in that path&rsquo;s part while it lasts. The curator&rsquo;s decisions of the day, P-171 on string literals and P-172 on numerals with a radix point, give the language&rsquo;s answers. The skill writer then changed the skill in two commits, at 15:32 and 15:37 UTC, the second with the fixes after a cold read. Their changes to the passages of changes 39 to 42, 58 and 67 are folded into those items, each with a third or second reason, &ldquo;Why, 8 November, afternoon&rdquo;. Change 40&rsquo;s and change 42&rsquo;s titles now say what their passages say. Change 68 is the one new item. None of these is a new rule: they correct the rules&rsquo; text, move the behaviour of one path into that path&rsquo;s part, or cut words that state no rule.</p>
<p>Of climb batch 28&rsquo;s items, two are new rules: change 51, how to convert an integer into another integer type; change 55, what to do in a shallow clone. One changes a rule: change 56, where the count stage&rsquo;s run after the second pass is now skipped when that pass changed no code. The other fifteen, and the change to change 43, state facts that the batch changed, define a term, fix a word or a citation, or cut text that states no rule.</p>
<p>Climb batch 29 landed on <code>main</code> on 9 November. It built the curator&rsquo;s decisions on numerals: a numeral converts where it stands, into the type that its place fixes or else into its default, and an expression of numerals alone folds exactly before the checker, on both paths. It also made two string literals of one text one object on both paths, and fixed the departures of walk that changes 39 to 42 named. So the landing made many sentences of the skill false. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 12:20, 12:24 and 12:34 UTC. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install three of its sections, with two corrections. Its changes to the passages of changes 39, 40 and 41 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 29&rdquo;, and changes 69 to 73 are its other changes. The second commit brought <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>compiler.md</code>, <code>tests-writing.md</code> and <code>library.md</code> in line with the landing. Its new lists of each path&rsquo;s departures from the rules on numbers stand in the passages of changes 42 and 68, so they are folded into those two items, and changes 74 to 83 are its other changes. A cold read of both commits then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed most of them in the third commit. Each item shows the net change of the three commits. Changes 40, 42 and 68 have new titles, since their passages now say other things.</p>
<p>Of climb batch 29&rsquo;s changes, two are new rules: the change to change 40, under which a numeral converts where it stands, in place of the rule that a numeral has a type of its own; and change 70, under which an expression of numerals alone folds exactly, on both paths. Change 81 puts the last sentence of change 70, that a test of run-time arithmetic needs a variable operand, into the part on tests, in place of a workaround for a defect that the batch fixed. The other thirteen new items, and the changes to changes 39, 41, 42 and 68, state facts that the batch changed, name departures and traps that it found, or fix a word, an example or a citation.</p>
<p>Climb batch 29b landed on <code>main</code> at 23:37 UTC on 9 November, as the repair batch after climb batch 29. It built the curator&rsquo;s decisions on a numeral&rsquo;s type: a numeral&rsquo;s type exists only at compile time, with the numeral&rsquo;s four numbers as its static arguments, and a number type takes a numeral through a coercion generic over those numbers, which gives one value for each place where the numeral is written. It took away the numeral types&rsquo; getters, and the compiler&rsquo;s prelude now follows the same design. So the landing made sentences of the skill false again. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 03:30, 03:32 and 03:42 UTC on 10 November. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install two of its sections, with two corrections. Its changes to the passages of changes 40, 41 and 70 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 29b&rdquo;, and changes 84 and 85 are its other changes. The second commit brought <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>compiler.md</code> and <code>library.md</code> in line with the landing. Its changes to the passages of changes 42, 68, 75 and 78 are folded into those items, and change 86 is its other change. A cold read of both commits then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed most of them in the third commit, which also took the second commit&rsquo;s sentence in <code>library.md</code> back out. Each item shows the net change of the three commits.</p>
<p>Of climb batch 29b&rsquo;s changes, five change or add rules: the change to change 40, under which a numeral&rsquo;s type has its four numbers as static arguments and a program&rsquo;s own number type takes a numeral through a coercion generic over them, in place of a coercion that reads getters; the change to change 41, which gives the rule&rsquo;s answer for <code>=</code> between a number and a numeral, and the form to write; the change to change 70, under which a folded constant reaches a program&rsquo;s own type only through a coercion from its default type; the change to change 75, under which a numeral type&rsquo;s numbers, used as values, are <code>ZZ</code> values; and change 86, under which the prelude&rsquo;s numerals are the one exception to the rule that adds nothing to the prelude. The others, changes 84 and 85 and the changes to changes 42, 68 and 78, state facts that the batch changed, name departures and traps that it found, or cut words that state no rule.</p>
<p>Climb batch 30 landed on <code>main</code> on 13 November. It made the compiled path run a call&rsquo;s arguments as parallel tasks, as the language&rsquo;s rule asks, fixed defects of the compiled <code>atomic</code> block and <code>try</code>, and fixed four departures on numerals that changes 40, 42 and 68 named (rows 958, 967, 971 and 974). So the landing made sentences of the skill false again. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, two at 22:41 and one at 22:46 UTC on 13 November. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install six of its seven sections, and held one, &ldquo;Static parameters, <code>where</code> clauses and sizes&rdquo;, which keeps its text. Beside the landing&rsquo;s one correction, on a numeral that rounds to an infinity, this update took in the findings of its own cold read, so it reworded sentences in each of the six sections. Its changes to the passages of changes 40, 41, 51, 70 and 85 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 30&rdquo;, and changes 87 to 100 are its other changes. The second commit brought <code>compiler.md</code>, <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>gate.md</code> and <code>tests-writing.md</code> in line with the landing. Its changes to the passages of changes 42, 68 and 78 are folded into those items, and changes 101 to 105 are its other changes. A cold read of both commits then found nineteen passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search. The skill writer fixed sixteen in the third commit and left three, which the items of changes 89, 96 and 98 name. Each item shows the net change of the three commits.</p>
<p>Of climb batch 30&rsquo;s changes, three are new rules: change 102, under which no compiled program relies on the order in which a call&rsquo;s arguments are evaluated; change 103, under which an <code>atomic</code> block or a <code>try</code> is bound in a statement of its own before it is passed as a later argument; and change 105, under which a test of parallelism under walk needs a range longer than 16. The change to change 40 adds the way to get an infinity. The other sixteen new items, and the changes to changes 41, 42, 51, 68, 70, 78 and 85, state facts that the batch changed, reword sentences that a reader could take two ways, or cut words that state no rule.</p>
<p>Climb batch 32 landed on <code>main</code> on 16 November. On both paths, it let a numeral run a more specific declaration than the one that fits its default type. It made the checker take the unwritten static argument of a reduction such as <code>SUM</code> from the reduction&rsquo;s expected type, or else from its body. It let a reference to a generic function take its instance from the arrow type that receives it. And it fixed walk&rsquo;s answer for <code>u = 0</code>, which changes 41 and 42 named (rows 973 and 966). So the landing made sentences of the skill false again. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 07:10, 07:13 and 07:19 UTC on 16 November. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install the three sections that it rewrote, &ldquo;Static parameters, <code>where</code> clauses and sizes&rdquo;, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo; and &ldquo;Reductions and comprehensions&rdquo;, and held none. Its changes to the passages of changes 41, 71 and 99 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 32&rdquo;, and changes 106 to 108 are its other changes. The second commit brought <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>compiler.md</code>, <code>gate.md</code>, <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>library.md</code> and <code>tests-writing.md</code> in line with the landing. Its changes to the passages of changes 42, 68 and 76 are folded into those items, and changes 109 to 117 are its other changes. A cold read of both commits then found twenty-three passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search. The skill writer fixed twenty-two in the third commit and left one, which the item of change 71 names. Each item shows the net change of the three commits. Changes 76 and 99 have new titles, since their passages now say other things.</p>
<p>Of climb batch 32&rsquo;s changes, three change or add rules of the language as built: change 108, under which a numeral can run a more specific declaration than the one that fits its default type; the change to change 71, under which a tie between declarations that apply only by coercion goes to the one that takes the call&rsquo;s numerals at their default; and the change to change 99, under which the checker takes a reduction&rsquo;s unwritten static argument from the expected type or the body, and a worker writes the argument at four places only. The change to change 76 states the rule of change 108 in <code>SKILL.md</code>. Change 116 changes an instruction: the element type of <code>SUM</code> and <code>PROD</code> is written in the library, and no longer everywhere. Change 117 is a new rule for the Fortress of a test: parenthesise a call or a subscript before a superscript. The other nine new items, and the changes to changes 41, 42 and 68, state facts that the batch changed, name departures and traps that it found, each with the way round it, or reword a sentence that a reader could take two ways.</p>
<p>Each item shows the skill&rsquo;s current text, as it stands on <code>main</code>, against the text before the item&rsquo;s first commit. When a later commit changes an item&rsquo;s passage, the change is folded into that item, with its own reason, and does not become a new item. The words that came in are underlined, and the words that went are struck through. The next new change will be change 118.</p>

<section id="l39" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 39 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;</span>A type without its own = compares by strict equivalence</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;, lines 57 to 64, a new rule after the rule on pairs and triples</p>
<p><span class="add"><strong>Where a type declares no <code>=</code>, <code>=</code> is strict equivalence, <code>SEQV</code> (P-168).</strong> Strict equivalence is the operator <code>&#x2263;</code> (STRICTLY EQUIVALENT TO), written <code>SEQV</code> or <code>===</code> in ASCII. The operator <code>&#x2261;</code> (IDENTICAL TO, <code>EQV</code> or <code>EQUIV</code>) is a different one: logical equivalence on booleans. The interpreter's library declares it for no type, and walk stops on it (row 184): write <code>a &lt;-&gt; b</code>. The library's <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code> answers <code>a SEQV b</code>. <code>SEQV</code> follows the specification on both paths:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span class="add">values of different types are never equivalent;</span></li>
<li><span class="add">a value object is equivalent field by field, a tuple element by element, an integer, a character or a boolean by its value, and a float by its bits;</span></li>
<li><span class="add">a reference object, a string among them, is equivalent only to itself;</span></li>
<li><span class="add">a function is equivalent to itself, but may be found unequal to another that behaves the same.</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span class="add">So for a value object <code>V</code> without its own <code>=</code>, <code>V(s) = V(t)</code> is <code>false</code> where <code>s</code> and <code>t</code> are equal strings made by two computations. Two string literals of one text are one object (P-171). So <code>"ab" SEQV "ab"</code> and <code>V("ab") = V("ab")</code> are <code>true</code>. Two string literals joined by <code>||</code> fold into one (&ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;), so <code>("a" || "b") SEQV "ab"</code> is <code>true</code>. A float numeral converts where <code>RR64</code> is declared (&ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;), so <code>y: RR64 = 0.5</code> is <code>SEQV</code> a computed <code>RR64</code> 0.5. The interpreter's library declares <code>=</code> on pairs and triples only, so <code>(s, 1, 2, 3) = (t, 1, 2, 3)</code> falls back to <code>SEQV</code> and is <code>false</code>; row 923 holds the question of longer tuples. To compare by content, give the value object an <code>=</code>, or compare the parts. A string's <code>=</code> compares text. Until the switch-over, the compiler's library declares no <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code> and none on tuples. Its own <code>&#x2263;</code>, declared as <code>===</code>, compares strings and <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code>, <code>RR32</code> and <code>RR64</code> by <code>=</code>, not by bits or identity (row 908).</span></p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule. The batch made both paths answer <code>SEQV</code>, strict equivalence, by the specification's rule, as the curator&rsquo;s decision on its meaning asks. The library's <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code>, which every type without its own <code>=</code> falls back to, answers <code>SEQV</code>. The old part said nothing of this. A worker would expect such an <code>=</code> to compare content, and would take the two <code>false</code> answers in the new text for defects. The new rule says how <code>SEQV</code> compares, gives those two cases and says to assert them as correct, and gives the fix. It also names walk's limit on <code>assert</code>, and what the compiler's library does until the switch-over. After the cold read, it says how an integer, a character or a boolean compares: by its value. It names a string's kind as &ldquo;an object not declared <code>value</code>&rdquo;, in place of &ldquo;a reference object&rdquo;, which the skill does not define. It writes the two <code>false</code> cases as examples, in place of a string &ldquo;built apart&rdquo;, which a worker could not apply. It points to walk's float numerals (change 42). And it says what <code>===</code> is: the ASCII spelling of <code>SEQV</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why, 8 November.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of the rule's meaning: what <code>SEQV</code> answers stays as the batch made it. The text now spells the operators out, and it adds three cases where a worker would guess wrong. The curator&rsquo;s comments on this change raised most of it. At 00:35 UTC on 8 November the curator asked to check the Unicode spelling of <code>SEQV</code> and <code>=/=</code>, and at 00:58 the curator asked that the text give both spellings and tell &ldquo;strictly equivalent to&rdquo; from &ldquo;identical to&rdquo;. The batch's text called <code>===</code> &ldquo;the ASCII spelling of <code>SEQV</code>&rdquo;, but <code>SEQV</code> is an ASCII spelling too, and the text never showed the characters. It now gives all three spellings of strict equivalence, <code>&#x2263;</code>, <code>SEQV</code> and <code>===</code>; it says that <code>&#x2261;</code> is another operator; and it says that <code>=/=</code> is <code>&#x2260;</code>. At 00:44 the curator asked that the skill use &ldquo;reference object&rdquo;, the term of the specification and of peer languages such as Swift. The batch's cold read had put &ldquo;an object not declared <code>value</code>&rdquo; in its place, since the skill did not define the term. Change 49 now defines it, so the bullet on strings says &ldquo;a reference object&rdquo;. A first wording of the fix called <code>&#x2261;</code> &ldquo;if and only if&rdquo; on booleans. The cold read ran it under walk, and walk stops on it, since the interpreter's library declares <code>&#x2261;</code> for no type. The text now calls it logical equivalence, the specification's words, and says to write <code>a &lt;-&gt; b</code>. The cold read also found two cases that the text left to a guess. Under walk, two equal string literals are two strings, so <code>"ab" SEQV "ab"</code> is <code>false</code>, where a worker who knows Java expects one shared string. And once change 49 took functions out of the reference objects, no bullet said what <code>SEQV</code> gives on two functions. A new bullet says it, from the specification.</p>
<p><strong>Why, 8 November, afternoon.</strong> Not a new rule: a correction of the rule&rsquo;s text. On 8 November the curator read changes 39 to 44 and refused them. They wrote the behaviour of one implementation as rules of the language, and told workers to assert it as correct. Here that was walk&rsquo;s new string object for each evaluation of a literal. By the curator&rsquo;s rule, <code>revival-changes.md</code> describes the language of revived Fortress, and the revival&rsquo;s changes against the team&rsquo;s design. A behaviour of one path that the language does not give is a defect, with a ledger row. While it lasts, that path&rsquo;s part, <code>interpreter.md</code> or <code>compiler.md</code>, notes it, never as a rule. The curator&rsquo;s decision of the day, P-171, gives the language&rsquo;s answer: two string literals of one text are one object, and a concatenation of two literals folds into one literal. So <code>"ab" SEQV "ab"</code> and <code>V("ab") = V("ab")</code> are now <code>true</code>. The second decision, P-172, makes a float numeral convert where <code>RR64</code> is declared, so <code>y: RR64 = 0.5</code> is now <code>SEQV</code> a computed <code>RR64</code> of the same value. The sentence &ldquo;Both <code>false</code> answers are the rule&rsquo;s: assert them as correct&rdquo; is gone. Ledger rows 943 and 945 now hold walk&rsquo;s departures from these answers, and row 945 the compiled path&rsquo;s, and the text points to the two parts that note them. The sentences on walk&rsquo;s <code>&#x2260;</code> and two-value <code>assert</code> left this rule: <code>tests-writing.md</code> keeps them (change 44). A cold read then fixed the wording. It names the fold&rsquo;s operator, <code>||</code>, with the example <code>("a" || "b") SEQV "ab"</code>, which is <code>true</code>. And it names the case of a tuple of four elements: the interpreter&rsquo;s library declares <code>=</code> on pairs and triples only, so such a tuple&rsquo;s <code>=</code> falls back to <code>SEQV</code> and answers <code>false</code>. Ledger row 923 holds the question of longer tuples.</p>
<p><strong>Why, climb batch 29.</strong> Not a new rule: the rule&rsquo;s answers stay, and two sentences follow what the batch fixed. Climb batch 29 made walk convert a float numeral where <code>RR64</code> is declared (row 943), and made two string literals of one text one object on both paths (row 945). So the sentence that named these two departures, and pointed to <code>interpreter.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> for them, is gone. Its example of a computed <code>RR64</code>, <code>0.25 + 0.25</code>, is now an expression of numerals alone, which the batch folds into one numeral before the run (change 70), so it no longer showed a computed value. The text now says &ldquo;a computed <code>RR64</code> 0.5&rdquo;. The sentence on two string literals joined by <code>||</code> now points to &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;, which states the fold, and its citation of the Working Draft moved to the skill&rsquo;s list of sources. The orientation update made these changes, and its judge said to install them. The cold read found nothing to fix here.</p></div>
