<p><strong><span class="cut">Under walk a numeral has a run-time class.</span><span class="add">A numeral converts where it stands (P-173).</span></strong> <span class="cut">The checker types an integer numeral as <code>IntLiteral</code>, a sibling under <code>Number</code> that converts to the integer types, <code>QQ</code> and <code>RR64</code> (P-76). Walk gives a numeral the class <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code> or <code>BigNum</code> by its value. These classes stand for <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, but a generic instantiates at the class: <code>Box[\Int\]</code> is not <code>Box[\ZZ32\]</code> (row 364). Walk answers <code>IntLiteral</code>'s arithmetic as a <code>ZZ32</code> (row 578).</span> <span class="add">A numeral's own type is <code>NaturalNumeral[\n, r, v\]</code>, or <code>RadixPointNumeral[\n, m, r, v\]</code> with a radix point (P-174). Its static arguments are the numeral's numbers: <code>n</code> the digit count, <code>m</code> the count of digits after the point, <code>r</code> the radix, and <code>v</code> the digits read as one integer in that radix, so <code>3.125</code> is a <code>RadixPointNumeral[\4, 3, 10, 3125\]</code>. Both lie below <code>Numeral</code>, a sibling of the number types under <code>Number</code>. No numeral value exists when a program runs, so a numeral type has no getters, no <code>asFloat</code>, no <code>asString</code> and no <code>=</code> with a number type. Until the switch-over, the compiler's prelude follows the same design (P-37).</span></p>
<p><span class="add">A place fixes a type when its declared type has a coercion from the numeral type, as each of the eight number types has. Such places are a typed binding, an assignment, a field, a parameter and a declared return type. At a number type, the numeral converts from its exact value: exactly into the five integer types and <code>QQ</code>, and with one rounding into <code>RR64</code> and <code>RR32</code> (P-76, P-172). So <code>u: NN32 = 5</code> binds, and <code>q: QQ = 0.1</code> is <code>1/10</code>. A numeral outside the type's range, or that rounds to an infinity there, is refused: by the checker statically, by walk when the place runs. Walk's message depends on the place: a top-level binding gives 'Type mismatch binding', a parameter 'got arg'. To get an infinity, overflow run-time arithmetic: for <code>t: RR64 = 10.0^308</code>, <code>t TIMES 10.0</code> is <code>Infinity</code> under walk. <code>x.isInfinite</code> tests for one. Until the switch-over, the compiler's prelude declares neither <code>TIMES</code> nor <code>isInfinite</code>.</span></p>
<p><span class="add">Elsewhere the numeral takes its default: <code>ZZ32</code>, or <code>ZZ64</code> or <code>ZZ</code> where its value does not fit, and <code>RR64</code> with a radix point. Elsewhere includes an untyped binding, <code>Any</code>, <code>Object</code>, <code>Number</code>, <code>Index</code> and an unbounded type parameter. So <code>n = 3000000000</code> is a <code>ZZ64</code>, and <code>Cell(0.5)</code> is a <code>Cell[\RR64\]</code>. The default stays the value's type: for <code>x = 0.1</code>, <code>q: QQ = x</code> is refused. A type parameter that numerals alone fix takes the narrowest default that holds them all. A <code>nat</code> or <code>int</code> parameter used as a value, other than a numeral type's number, is a <code>ZZ32</code>, so <code>u: NN32 = n</code> is refused (P-173, P-40). Such a parameter of 2^31 or more is a <code>ZZ64</code> under walk, and raises an error on the compiled path (row 962).</span></p>
